//
// loft#1443 — writing a closure into a `&fn(…) -> τ` PARAMETER.
//
// `@FR-B-Ref-Intro` gives `&τ` for EVERY τ, with no τ excluded — the register says so in as
// many words, having closed `D-bind-17` on exactly that sentence one day before this defect
// was filed (loft#1372: a `&τ?` link, where what was missing was "one spelling of the SLOT
// behind a link, at the nine sites that each asked the link's inner type bare").  A fn-typed
// link is the next such spelling, and it was an internal compiler error on both backends:
// `Unknown reference variable type`, from the write-back's type allow-list.
//
// An ICE is never the right answer, and the direction is not a design call: refusing `&fn(…)`
// at the parameter would be a fresh deviation against a rule closed the day before.  The
// allow-list that panicked already carried the precedent in its own comment — loft#1292 added
// the keyed kinds to it because "an allow-list whose omission costs an ICE rather than an
// optimisation is the trade the other way round".
//
// A fn-ref has TWO representations and the write needs the STACK one: 20 bytes, 8 B `d_nr`
// plus a 12 B closure `DbRef`.  (A struct FIELD holds the packed heap form — a 4-byte d_nr and
// a child record — so the field write that already worked was the wrong model to copy.)  No op
// wrote 20 bytes through a link, so `OpSetStackFnRef` does it the way `&text` already does:
// read the link at the frame slot, then write where it points.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  what the closure captures (nothing · a struct · a nullable struct ·
//                        an integer · a text), whether it captures at ALL, whether the source
//                        is a bare fn NAME (a fn-ref with an empty environment) or a lambda,
//                        the fn's arity and return type, the number of writes through one
//                        link, and whether the source is an if-VALUED expression.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the link is a PARAMETER.  A `&fn(…)` LOCAL bind (`pd: &fn() -> integer
//                        = &g`) is NOT this defect and is deliberately excluded: the `&` bind
//                        lowering admits `Reference`/`Tuple`/`Text` and not `Function`, so the
//                        link is never installed and the bind cannot be told from a write.
//                        That is a second unsound step in its own site, it still ICEs here as
//                        it always did, and it is loft#1454 — filed rather than half-fixed,
//                        because admitting the fn source there makes the INTERPRETER right and
//                        leaves `--native` emitting an empty initialiser and a non-deref write.
//
// ── The second half: the record the write HANDS OVER ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Making the write compile was not the whole fix.  A capturing closure's environment lives in
// a `__closure_N` store record the callee minted, and `scopes.rs` decided the frame's exit
// frees by asking whether the value is RETURNED — the only route out of a frame before this
// issue existed.  A link is a second route, so the callee freed the record it had just handed
// to the caller, and the free cascaded into the capture: the caller then called a closure over
// `0xDEADBEEF`.  `record_leaves_frame`'s doc comment had reserved the place for it in as many
// words — *"when it is implemented this predicate gains a second source and must be told"* —
// and loft#1443 did not tell it.
//
// ⚠ **Every cell below passed while that was broken.**  A freed arena slot still reads back
// the bytes it held, so the assertions read the right values off a dead record.  Measured on
// the pre-fix build: `13 passed` with no poison, and `8 failed, 5 passed` under
// `LOFT_POISON=1`, which fills a freed record with `0xDEADBEEF`.  `LOFT_STRICT_STORES=1` on
// that same build printed twenty `USE AFTER FREE` lines and the run still reported `13
// passed` — it names the fault without failing the run.  So these cells are not
// self-sufficient: this file is a poison-gate guard as much as a value guard, and a green
// `loft test` over it says nothing about the lifetime half.
//
// Axes the lifetime half MOVES:  how the closure reaches the link (written straight in · through
//                        a fn-ref LOCAL, whose own scope-exit free cascades into the same
//                        record), how many records one frame mints and delivers (one · two
//                        through the same link, where only the LAST reaches the caller and the
//                        displaced one is still this frame's to free), and whether the frame
//                        delivers by BOTH routes at once (a return and a link write).
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the caller's slot holds a NON-capturing closure before the write.
//                        Moving it is a `--native` wrong answer that predates this file — the
//                        write does not land at all, `interpret` says 88 and `--native` says
//                        99 — and it is loft#1463, measured on the build before this fix as
//                        well as after.
//
// @falsified-at: f5cd8a37e — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 101 -> 0.  On the control EVERY
//   cell is the ICE, so the file cannot even reach an assertion there; each cell's VALUE is
//   hand-computed below and checked on both backends, which is what makes the pass mean
//   something rather than the two binaries agreeing with each other.
// @falsified-at: 8e242d34 (the lifetime half, under `LOFT_POISON=1`) — interpret 8 assertion
//   failures -> 0, 13 passed both times WITHOUT poison.  That second number is the point: the
//   control build is wrong and silent, and only the poison sweep separates the two.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct S1443 { a: integer }

fn nine1443() -> integer { 9 }
fn one1443() -> integer { 1 }
fn two1443() -> integer { 2 }

// --------------------------------------------------- the defect: a write through the link

fn give_capturing(out: &fn() -> integer) {
  d: S1443 = S1443 { a: 7 };
  out = fn() -> integer { d.a };
}

fn test_a_capturing_closure_reaches_the_caller() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_capturing(g);
  assert(g() == 7, "the caller calls the closure the callee wrote");
}

// A NON-capturing lambda carries only the d_nr, where a capturing one already carries the
// pair — the axis the issue's own reproducer holds fixed, and the one `--native` failed on
// after the interpreter was right.
fn give_non_capturing(out: &fn() -> integer) { out = fn() -> integer { 42 }; }

fn test_a_non_capturing_closure_reaches_the_caller() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_non_capturing(g);
  assert(g() == 42, "a closure with an empty environment is written whole");
}

// A bare fn NAME is `(L-FnRef)`'s fn-ref: a closure with an empty environment.  It also has to
// stay REACHABLE — a function reached only through a link was pruned, leaving the native
// dispatch without an arm and the call panicking `invalid fn-ref`.
fn give_fn_ref(out: &fn() -> integer) { out = nine1443; }

fn test_a_bare_fn_name_reaches_the_caller() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_fn_ref(g);
  assert(g() == 9, "a fn-ref written through a link is still reachable");
}

fn give_nullable_capture(out: &fn() -> integer) {
  d: S1443? = S1443 { a: 5 };
  out = fn() -> integer { if d == null { -1 } else { d.a } };
}

fn test_a_nullable_capture_is_not_the_axis() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_nullable_capture(g);
  assert(g() == 5, "nullability of the capture does not enter into it");
}

fn give_scalar_capture(out: &fn() -> integer) { n: integer = 3; out = fn() -> integer { n + 1 }; }

fn test_a_scalar_capture_reaches_the_caller() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_scalar_capture(g);
  assert(g() == 4, "a scalar capture is written like any other");
}

// Arity and return type are the fn TYPE's own, and the link carries whatever it names.
fn give_with_argument(out: &fn(integer) -> integer) {
  k: integer = 10;
  out = fn(x: integer) -> integer { x + k };
}

fn test_a_parameterised_fn_type_reaches_the_caller() {
  g: fn(integer) -> integer = fn(x: integer) -> integer { x };
  give_with_argument(g);
  assert(g(5) == 15, "an argument-taking closure is written whole");
}

fn give_text_returning(out: &fn() -> text) { s: text = "hi"; out = fn() -> text { s }; }

fn test_a_text_returning_closure_reaches_the_caller() {
  g: fn() -> text = fn() -> text { "" };
  give_text_returning(g);
  assert(g() == "hi", "the return type is the fn type's, not the link's");
}

// TWO writes through one link: the second must land, and the first must not be read after it.
fn give_twice(out: &fn() -> integer) {
  out = fn() -> integer { 1 };
  out = fn() -> integer { 2 };
}

fn test_the_last_write_through_a_link_wins() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_twice(g);
  assert(g() == 2, "a link written twice holds the second closure");
}

// An if-VALUED source is why the pair is asked for through the shared context rather than by
// wrapping the emitted expression: a wrap around the whole `if` leaves both ARMS bare.
fn give_if_valued(out: &fn() -> integer, pick: boolean) {
  out = if pick { one1443 } else { two1443 };
}

fn test_an_if_valued_source_builds_the_pair_in_each_arm() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_if_valued(g, false);
  assert(g() == 2, "the else arm's fn-ref is written");
  h: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_if_valued(h, true);
  assert(h() == 1, "and the then arm's");
}

// ── the record the write hands over ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The closure reaches the link through a fn-ref LOCAL rather than being written straight in.
// Two frees aim at one record here — the record's own scope-exit free and the CASCADE that
// `h`'s free triggers — and suppressing only the first leaves the second to destroy it.
fn give_via_local(out: &fn() -> integer) {
  d: S1443 = S1443 { a: 21 };
  h = fn() -> integer { d.a };
  out = h;
}

fn test_a_closure_reaches_the_link_through_a_local() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_via_local(g);
  assert(g() == 21, "a closure handed to the link through a local: {g()}");
}

// TWO capturing writes through one link.  The caller holds the SECOND, so the first is this
// frame's to free after all — suppressing both hands the caller one record and leaks the
// other.  `test_the_last_write_through_a_link_wins` is this cell's non-capturing twin and
// cannot see the difference: with no captures there is no record either way.
fn give_twice_capturing(out: &fn() -> integer) {
  d: S1443 = S1443 { a: 1 };
  out = fn() -> integer { d.a };
  e: S1443 = S1443 { a: 2 };
  out = fn() -> integer { e.a };
}

fn test_the_displaced_capturing_write_is_still_this_frames_to_free() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  give_twice_capturing(g);
  assert(g() == 2, "the second capturing write is what the caller holds: {g()}");
}

// BOTH routes out of one frame at once.  The two must union rather than take turns: asking
// only the returns would free the record the link handed over.
fn give_both_ways(out: &fn() -> integer) -> fn() -> integer {
  d: S1443 = S1443 { a: 6 };
  out = fn() -> integer { d.a };
  e: S1443 = S1443 { a: 60 };
  fn() -> integer { e.a }
}

fn test_a_frame_delivers_by_both_routes_at_once() {
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  r = give_both_ways(g);
  assert(g() == 6, "the link-written closure: {g()}");
  assert(r() == 60, "and the returned one, from the same frame: {r()}");
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- the control

// The same write with NO link: unchanged by all of this, and the cell that says so.
fn test_a_plain_fn_local_assignment_is_unchanged() {
  d: integer = 7;
  g: fn() -> integer = fn() -> integer { 0 };
  g = fn() -> integer { d };
  assert(g() == 7, "a fn local without a link keeps taking its own path");
}
